  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 306 AM CDT Tue Oct 27 2026\n\nLatest water vapor imagery this morning shows a subtle shortwave \nworking its way into eastern Kansas as a deeper shortwave pushes \ninto the northern Rockies/Plains. The shortwave over Kansas has \ndeepened an inverted trough over central and northeastern Kansas \nthat has increased low-level isentropic ascent across the CWA. Light \nshowers continue within this corridor of ascent. As the shortwave \nslowly moves into western Missouri later this morning, subsidence \nbehind the wave should bring a quick end to precipitation. Given the \nample cloud cover and weak troughing across the area, temperatures \nwill not warm much this afternoon, topping out in the lower 70s with \nsome upper 60s across far north-central Kansas. Overnight tonight, \nthe shortwave over the northern Rockies region begins to move into \nthe WY Basin with subtle mid-level energy embedded in the southwest \nflow to its south. The subtle cooling aloft ahead of the shortwave \naxis and low-level isentropic ascent may be enough to develop some \nisolated to scattered showers/weak thunderstorms in the early \nmorning hours Wednesday. \n\nThe shortwave over the northern Plains will continue to track east \nthrough the day Wednesday and help to push a lee surface trough \nahead through the region. As the surface trough moves across the \nPlains, tight pressure gradients will return gusty southerly flow \nacross Kansas and push a corridor of 60 degree Tds into eastern \nKansas. The surface boundary tracks into northern Kansas by the \nlater afternoon/evening creating a focal point for showers and \nstorms. Storms that do develop along the front in the evening will \nlikely remain sub-severe given the lack of deep shear aloft, but \ngiven RAP analysis depicting 1000-1500 J/kg of MLCAPE, a few storms \ncould briefly be on the stronger side. \n\nPersistent rain/storm chances (30-60%) look to continue again \nThursday night into Friday morning as the surface boundary stalls \nnear the KS/NE border. With warm and moist southerly flow overriding \nthe boundary, widespread showers will be expected. This will quickly \nbe follow up with the next system moving in from the southern \nPlains, keeping rain and storm chances across the region into \nSaturday (50-70%). Given the precipitation chances and ample cloud \ncover, temperatures will not warm much above the low 70 Saturday \ninto next week. The active pattern appears to remain in place as we \nhead into next week. That said, some dry weather should find eastern \nKansas by Saturday night into most of Sunday as subsidence and mid-\nlevel ridging build in from the southwest.",
